        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "I've hit this a few times so I added some code to print more information when this occurs:\n```patch\ndiff --git a/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/sync.lua b/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/sync.lua\nindex 36a94d5fba..731fd7b118 100644\n--- a/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/sync.lua\n+++ b/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/sync.lua\n@@ -192,6 +192,9 @@ local function compute_end_range(\n   -- A special case for the following `firstline == new_lastline` case where lines are deleted.\n   -- Even if the buffer has become empty, nvim behaves as if it has an empty line with eol.\n   if #curr_lines == 1 and curr_lines[1] == '' then\n+    if not (prev_lines[lastline - 1]) then\n+      error(string.format(\"lastline[%s] #prev_lines[%s] prev_lines: k[%s] v[%s]\", lastline, #prev_lines, next(prev_lines)))\n+    end\n     local prev_line = assert(prev_lines[lastline - 1])\n     return {\n       line_idx = lastline - 1,\n```\n\nI hit it today while running [`:Merge`](https://github.com/idbrii/vim-mergetool) which modifies the buffer for a git merge (it creates a diff of the conflicts):\n```\nError in function\nmergetool#toggle[4]\nmergetool#start[46]\nmergetool#prefer_revision[4]\n<SNR>753_restore_merged_file_contents[1]\nLua callback: $VIMRUNTIME/lua/vim/lsp/sync.lua:196: lastline[757] #prev_lines[722] prev_lines: k[1] v[local Loader = require \"loader\"]\nstack traceback:\n\t[C]: in function 'error'\n\t$VIMRUNTIME/lua/vim/lsp/sync.lua:196: in function 'compute_end_range'\n\t$VIMRUNTIME/lua/vim/lsp/sync.lua:409: in function 'compute_diff'\n\t$VIMRUNTIME/lua/vim/lsp/_changetracking.lua:106: in function 'incremental_changes'\n\t$VIMRUNTIME/lua/vim/lsp/_changetracking.lua:361: in function 'send_changes_for_group'\n\t$VIMRUNTIME/lua/vim/lsp/_changetracking.lua:398: in function 'send_changes'\n\t$VIMRUNTIME/lua/vim/lsp.lua:955: in function $VIMRUNTIME/lua/vim/lsp.lua:949>\n```\n\n\nHere's the vim code that's triggering the sync:\n```vimscript\n\" Discard all changes in buffer, and fill it with original merged file contents\nfunction! s:restore_merged_file_contents()\n  %delete _ | put =s:mergedfile_contents | 1delete _\nendfunction\n```\n\nIt was opening quest.lua and `local Loader = require \"loader\"` is the first line of that file.\n\nOn disk:\n```\n    wc quest.lua\n      756  2186 22702 C:\\code\\quest.lua\n    file quest.lua\n    C:\\code\\quest.lua: JavaScript source, ASCII text, with CRLF line terminators\n```\n\nIn buffer:\n```\n    wc quest.lua\n      742  2114 21927 C:\\code\\quest.lua\n    file quest.lua\n    C:\\code\\quest.lua: JavaScript source, ASCII text, with CRLF line terminators\n```\n\nThe output shows that the assert would fail because the input `lastline` (which appears to come from the on-disk file length) is after the last line in `prev_lines` (which are shorter than any version of the file??). However, I'm not sure what it should return in the case where we're looking for changes that in lines that no longer exist.\n\n\n\nI'm on the last stable release:\n    NVIM v0.12.4\n    Build type: Release\n    LuaJIT 2.1.1774638290\n\nBut this compute_end_range issue is very sporadic and I only see it about once a month (last time was also from vim-mergetool). Looks like sync.lua hasn't changed in master, so hopefully this information is still relevant.\n\n\nI'll try running with bechampion's patch for awhile. \n",
